EXT. PIER 9 - PARKED ICE-CREAM TRUCK - NIGHT

Harbor lights. Gulls asleep on bollards. A white truck with a cartoon cone on its side, awnings rolled, serving window shuttered. Engine ticking as it cools.

Inside, a single dome light.

RENATA GUILLORY, 40s, wears a chef's apron over a leather jacket. Her wrists are zip-tied to the steering wheel. Blood in one eyebrow. She looks at the man in the passenger seat.

VIKTOR LAZAREV, 50s, cleans a pistol with an ice-cream napkin. Salt-and-pepper beard. Soft Russian accent. Very calm.

RENATA
You can't be serious.

VIKTOR
I am always serious. It is my curse.

RENATA
You're stealing a truck full of Rocky Road.

VIKTOR
I am recovering a truck full of something else.

RENATA
There's nothing else.

VIKTOR
The second freezer.

RENATA
That's for the sprinkles.

VIKTOR
Renata.

RENATA
Fine. It's for the other thing.

VIKTOR
Which is?

RENATA
Four kilos. You know it's four kilos.

VIKTOR
I know it was five.

RENATA
Four.

VIKTOR
You took one.

RENATA
I took what I was owed.

VIKTOR
By whom?

RENATA
By you. You didn't pay me for the last three runs.

VIKTOR
That is not how it works.

RENATA
That is exactly how it works, Viktor. I carry. You pay. This week you didn't.

VIKTOR
I was in Odessa.

RENATA
You were in a hotel with a woman.

VIKTOR
My wife.

RENATA
Your other wife.

A pause. A gull calls from far off. The water slaps against the pilings.

VIKTOR
Who told you?

RENATA
I make ice cream. People talk to ice-cream women.

VIKTOR
(sighing)
I am tired, Renata.

RENATA
Then go home.

VIKTOR
I would like to. But I have a partner who says four when it is five.

RENATA
And a partner who says five when it is four.

VIKTOR
You have a point.

RENATA
Then cut me loose.

VIKTOR
Why?

RENATA
So I can show you where the fifth kilo is.

VIKTOR
Why would you do that?

RENATA
Because I'm tired too.

Long silence. He puts down the napkin. Puts down the gun on the dashboard, barrel pointed at the windshield. Produces a pocket knife and, without looking at her, saws through the tie on her left wrist.

VIKTOR
The other one when I see it.

RENATA
Fair.

She flexes her freed hand. Reaches under the seat, slowly, and pulls out a battered tin lunchbox. Opens it. Inside, wrapped in butcher paper, a brick the size of a loaf of bread.

VIKTOR
That's it?

RENATA
That's it.

VIKTOR
Why did you keep it?

RENATA
It was insurance.

VIKTOR
Against me?

RENATA
Against anyone.

Viktor picks up the brick. Weighs it in his palm. Looks out at the dark harbor.

VIKTOR
You know, in Odessa my grandmother sold ice cream from a bicycle.

RENATA
Is that true?

VIKTOR
No.

He puts the brick back in the lunchbox. Closes it. Sets it between them on the console.

VIKTOR (CONT'D)
Keep it.

RENATA
What?

VIKTOR
Keep it. Pay yourself. Call it back wages.

RENATA
What's the catch?

VIKTOR
No catch. I am retiring.

RENATA
You can't retire.

VIKTOR
I have been retiring for six years. This is the first time anyone believed me.

He cuts the other tie. She rubs her wrists. He holds out his hand. She stares at it. Takes it.

RENATA
Then what do I call you?

VIKTOR
Viktor. A man who sells ice cream from a bicycle.

RENATA
A bicycle.

VIKTOR
Of course.

He opens the door. Steps out into the harbor air. Doesn't look back. The door shuts with a soft thud. The dome light goes dark.

Renata sits in the quiet, the lunchbox beside her, the engine finally silent. She opens the serving window. A cold wind off the water. She breathes.
